WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:03.740
- [Narrator] When you think
of Sweden, what comes to mind?

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.050
Ikea, Abba?

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.620
Maybe those delicious cinnamon rolls?

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.250
But what about these?

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:11.800
Swedish meatballs.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:12.633
Right?

00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:14.690
Well, maybe not.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:17.163
Welcome to the meatball mystery.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.110
Let's cut straight to the chase.

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:24.160
It's hard to make the Swedes
angry, but on one fateful day,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.720
something happened that
fired up the whole country.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.370
This tweet was sent from the official

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:30.800
Sweden Twitter account:

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.610
Swedish meatballs are
actually based on a recipe

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.280
from King Charles XII
brought back from Turkey

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:35.320
in the early 18th century.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.370
Let's stick to the facts.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.203
Yikes.

00:00:38.203 --> 00:00:39.130
Did you catch that?

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:40.740
The tweet said that Swedish meatballs

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.840
actually come from Turkey.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.910
As you can imagine, the tweet went viral.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.700
Swedes were up in arms.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.883
It was picked up all around the world.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.820
- The Swedish government has admitted

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.282
Swedish meatballs are actually Turkish.

00:00:55.282 --> 00:00:56.740
What?

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:57.690
What?

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.410
- No, that's not a fact.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.090
The fact is really interesting.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.370
Since we don't have any
facts from that period.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:05.720
- [Narrator] Wait, who are you?

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.390
- My name is Richard Tellström.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.010
I'm a food historian.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:10.550
- [Narrator] OK, here's what we know.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:14.210
In the 1700s, Swedish King
Charles XXII lost a battle

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:15.920
in the Great Northern War.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.950
He was forced to flee
to the Ottoman Empire

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.987
where for several years
he negotiated his return.

00:01:20.987 --> 00:01:23.770
The controversial tweets
suggested that he brought back

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.890
a local Turkish meat dish called koftas.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:29.539
- However, there is no
documented interests

00:01:29.539 --> 00:01:31.530
from him in food.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.850
No mentioning of the meatballs there.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.530
They are not Turkish
because we can't follow

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.580
the original meatballs in the world.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.270
There is no zero meatball
where everything started.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:47.450
Most likely, origin of the
meatball is a development

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:48.830
in parallel ways-

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.040
in parallel food cultures.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.110
Therefore, meatballs are very
different all over the world,

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:57.000
but they exist all over the world.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.910
- [Narrator] Today it's one of
Sweden's most popular dishes.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.817
Traditionally served with
mashed potatoes, a brown gravy,

00:02:02.817 --> 00:02:05.280
and a sweet lingonberry sauce.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.887
Mm-mm.

00:02:06.887 --> 00:02:11.102
- [Richard] Food gives us an
example of how we are connected

00:02:11.102 --> 00:02:13.020
when it comes to culture.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.250
Food is a sort of cultural network.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:18.810
- [Narrator] So take pride
in your meatballs, Sweden.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.950
And so should Turkey, China, Italy,

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.300
and every meatball rolling country.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.890
Because if there's one thing
the world can agree on,

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.603
it's that meatballs are delicious.

